// Numeric literal range (PHASE 6.5 follow-up).
//
// `strtod` reports ERANGE both for overflow and for underflow, but a
// representable subnormal is a valid literal. A `double` literal is rejected
// only when it overflows to infinity (`1e400`) or underflows all the way to
// zero (`1e-400`); a subnormal such as `4.9e-324` (the smallest `double`) is
// accepted. A `float` literal is narrowed to binary32 first, so `1e-45f` (the
// smallest `float` subnormal) is accepted while `1e-320f` (which would flush to
// zero) is rejected, matching Java's compile-time range check.

// `double` subnormals are accepted and agree with the runtime conversion.
assertEq(1e-320, double("1e-320"));
assertEq(4.9e-324, double("4.9e-324"));
assertEq(1e-323, double("1e-323"));
assertEq(type(1e-320), "double");

// The smallest normal double is unaffected.
assertEq(1e-308, double("1e-308"));

// `float` subnormals are accepted and narrow to binary32.
assertEq(1e-45f, float("1e-45"));
assertEq(type(1e-45f), "float");

// A literal that is exactly zero is never out of range, however large the
// exponent, because the mantissa is zero.
assertEq(0e400, 0.0);
assertEq(0.0e-400, 0.0);

print("float literal range ok");
